\cf2 \
You have a third nerve palsy in one eye. This means the third cranial nerve, which controls several eye muscles, the eyelid lifting muscle, and sometimes the pupil, is not working normally.\
\
A third nerve palsy can cause double vision, a drooping eyelid, difficulty moving the eye in multiple directions, and in some cases a larger pupil. Because the third nerve controls several important functions, this type of nerve palsy needs careful assessment.\
\
Some third nerve palsies are caused by reduced blood flow to the nerve, but others can be caused by compression, including from an aneurysm. That is why urgent medical evaluation is often recommended, especially when the pupil is involved or the pattern is new.\
\
